Funny about the Giants, if you're up on your baseball lore, but they
always had a kid who was gonna be another Mel Ott.

There was the Texas kid named Clint Hartung who came after the war. For a service team, he had hit
556 and threw no-hitters every day. The Giants couldn’t wait to turn him loose on the National League.
A year later in spring training he was struggling to make the team. A columnist for a New York paper
wrote of Hartung’s difficulties and heartbreak.
The Giants broke camp and moved through the midwest on a exhibition tour. Everywhere they stopped, the
Hartung story would be reprinted in the local journal.

Hartung had seen the piece maybe 20 times and was sick of it. Finally, in an Oklahoma City hotel lobby he
grabbed the New York writer by the lapels and shouted, ‘‘When the hell you gonna stop writing that column about me?”
Andy burned out
There was another immortal of an earlier time, an inspiration of the team’s famous manager, John
McGraw. For years he dreamed of having a Jewish star as a lure for New York's growing Jewish population.
Into his arms came Andy Cohen. The Giants made room for this new wonder by trading Rogers Hornsby 
to Boston. His first game, Cohen won the game with a double.
The next year, Cohen was gone. The legend is that Jewish society distracted him with so many invitations
he could no longer concentrate on baseball. As Cohen went to the minors, Hornsby hit .387 for Boston.
The Giants always had a way of disappearing.

